{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793861942,"nonce":"ERFNwD0q32epMBSCcW2ZBKNDBvE=","title":"Structural recursion over recursive types","message":"Deferred from M5a (ticket 8958455, comment #5 amended; owner confirmed 2026-11-04). Milestone: M7 (eef5771), alongside the heap model, the first place C# types can be recursive. Parent epic: 54c6e06f383039f9758f1571999c683db1c244561561998cf9a52c41b84fa52f.\n\nM5a translates every self-recursive method to well-founded recursion with Spec.Decreases (WellFounded.Nat.fix over Int.toNat of the measure, a decrease theorem per call, and a generated equation lemma; docs/m5a/03). Structural recursion was not built because no M5a type can recurse structurally: Int32 and Int64 have no brecOn (termination_by structural n fails in Lean 4.35.0), and records and [Closed] types cannot be recursive.\n\nWhen recursive types arrive (a linked list or tree class, a recursive record if records gain them), translate recursion that descends into a structurally smaller argument through the type's brecOn, as Lean's structural recursion does, and generate its equation lemmas. Design §4.2's \"structural recursion when detectable\" is the target. Acceptance: a method recursing over a recursive C# type translates without Spec.Decreases, its equation lemma is kernel-checked, and differential tests pass for it.","files":null}]}